STATEMENT 3: InsertProductAsync
================================================================================
Original MS SQL Statement:
DECLARE @NewProductId INT;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
    INSERT INTO Products (Name, Description, Price, StockQuantity)
    VALUES (@Name, @Description, @Price, @StockQuantity);
    
    SET @NewProductId = SCOPE_IDENTITY();
    
    INSERT INTO ProductHistory (ProductId, Action, OldPrice, NewPrice, OldStock, NewStock, ActionDate)
    VALUES (@NewProductId, 'INSERT', NULL, @Price, NULL, @StockQuantity, GETDATE());
    
    UPDATE ProductStats
    SET 
        TotalProducts = TotalProducts + 1,
        AveragePrice = (AveragePrice * TotalProducts + @Price) / (TotalProducts + 1),
        LastUpdated = GETDATE()
    WHERE StatId = 1;
COMMIT;

SELECT @NewProductId;

DMS Tool Output:
{
  "status": "error",
  "error": "Metadata model creation failed: {'error': 'Unknown metadata model creation status: RECEIVED'}"
}

Manual Conversion Applied:
Key PostgreSQL Changes:
1. BEGIN TRANSACTION → BEGIN
2. SCOPE_IDENTITY() → Use RETURNING clause on INSERT
3. GETDATE() → CURRENT_TIMESTAMP (2 occurrences)
4. COMMIT → COMMIT (compatible)
5. Use RETURNING ProductId on the INSERT statement to get the new ID
6. Use LASTVAL() in subsequent INSERT to get the last inserted sequence value

Reasoning:
- PostgreSQL uses RETURNING clause instead of SCOPE_IDENTITY()
- CURRENT_TIMESTAMP is the PostgreSQL standard for getting current date/time
- Transaction syntax differs (BEGIN vs BEGIN TRANSACTION)

